    level: "C1",
    text: "Spirits — also called liquor — are alcoholic beverages produced by distillation, concentrating the alcohol content far beyond what fermentation alone can achieve. The six major spirit categories form the backbone of every bar. Gin — Britain's national spirit — is a neutral grain spirit redistilled with botanicals, principally juniper berries. London Dry Gin is the classic style, though contemporary gins experiment with everything from rhubarb to seaweed. Vodka, originally from Eastern Europe, is valued for its purity and neutrality — a clean canvas for cocktails. Rum is distilled from sugarcane or molasses — light rum for mixing, dark rum for sipping, and spiced rum for warming winter cocktails. Whisky (spelled without an 'e' in Scotland, with an 'e' in Ireland — 'whiskey') is grain spirit aged in oak barrels; Scotch whisky — single malt or blended — is one of Britain's most valuable exports. Tequila and mezcal, from Mexico's agave plant, have moved from shots to sophisticated sipping spirits. Brandy — distilled from wine — includes Cognac and Armagnac from France. Liqueurs differ from spirits: they are sweetened and flavoured — examples include Cointreau (orange), Amaretto (almond), Baileys (cream), and Chartreuse (herbal). Understanding spirits is essential for anyone interested in cocktail culture, hospitality, or simply ordering confidently at a bar.",
